            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Works both ways. Give it a list and it makes a square matrix with the list down its diagonal and zeros elsewhere. Give it a matrix and it reads the diagonal off as a list.\n\nA diagonal matrix scales each axis separately - diag of 2 1 0.5 stretches x, leaves y and squashes z - so it is the way to build a scaling for np.matmul. Reading the diagonal gets the variances out of a covariance matrix, the ones from a correlation check, or the strength of each axis.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.diag <diagonal: int>\nDefault: 0, the main diagonal.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA list (to make a matrix) or a 2-D matrix (to read a diagonal). Every input is converted and sent.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich diagonal: 0 the main one, 1 the one above it, -1 the one below. Making a matrix with diagonal 1 makes it one larger, to leave room.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndiag out:\nThe matrix, or the list.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 becomes a 3 x 3 matrix with 1 2 3 down the diagonal; a second np.diag reads them back off. A metro re-sends the list five times a second; edit it and click.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.eye makes a diagonal of ones. np.cov puts variances on its diagonal. t.diag is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Works both ways. Give it a list and it makes a square matrix with the list down its diagonal and zeros elsewhere. Give it a matrix and it reads the diagonal off as a list.\n\nA diagonal matrix scales each axis separately - diag of 2 1 0.5 stretches x, leaves y and squashes z - so it is the way to build a scaling for np.matmul. Reading the diagonal gets the variances out of a covariance matrix, the ones from a correlation check, or the strength of each axis.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.diag <diagonal: int>\nDefault: 0, the main diagonal.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA list (to make a matrix) or a 2-D matrix (to read a diagonal). Every input is converted and sent.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich diagonal: 0 the main one, 1 the one above it, -1 the one below. Making a matrix with diagonal 1 makes it one larger, to leave room.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndiag out:\nThe matrix, or the list.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 becomes a 3 x 3 matrix with 1 2 3 down the diagonal; a second np.diag reads them back off. A metro re-sends the list five times a second; edit it and click.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.eye makes a diagonal of ones. np.cov puts variances on its diagonal. t.diag is the PyTorch version."
        },
